+# Privacy baseline and deployment
+
+The site collects no application data and ships no client JavaScript. Do not add a consent banner while there is nothing optional to consent to. The public notice is in `privacy-policy.html`.
+
+## Third-party inventory
+
+| Component | Visitor data flow | Decision |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Coffee photograph from Unsplash | Served locally as `assets/coffee.jpg`; no visitor request to Unsplash | Remote background replaced. Original source: https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1495474472287-4d71bcdd2085 |
+| Fonts and CSS | System fonts and local CSS only | No external font service |
+| Analytics, advertising, embeds, SDKs | None | Keep disabled by default |
+| Hosting provider | Receives IP, request path, browser headers; may retain logs | Deployment owner must review provider access, subprocessors, log redaction and retention |
+| `pnpm dlx statikk` start command | Development tooling; fetches an unpinned third-party package when invoked | Not shipped to browsers; not a production security configuration. Review/pin tooling before production use |
+
+Run `npm run audit:privacy` on every change. This dependency-free static guard checks the current deny-by-default baseline, HTML, CSS, asset URLs and new client code/dependencies. It is not a vulnerability scanner or proof of host configuration. Any new third party needs a documented purpose, data inventory, retention/deletion policy, hosting/subprocessor review, and assessment of whether local delivery avoids disclosure. Review this inventory when assets, dependencies, or hosting change.
+
+## Security headers
+
+`_headers` configures hosts supporting Netlify/Cloudflare Pages header syntax. Publish it with the HTML and assets. On other hosts, translate every directive into the host's actual response-header configuration. The existing development start command does not establish these production headers. Do not assume headers are active merely because this file exists.
+
+Serve over HTTPS and redirect HTTP to HTTPS at the host. HSTS applies for one year to this hostname. CSP allows local images and CSS, blocks scripts, outbound connections, forms, frames, objects and workers, and prohibits framing by other sites. Referrers are suppressed, browser capabilities are restricted, content sniffing is disabled, and responses use `no-store`. The HTML CSP and referrer meta tags provide a fallback; frame restrictions, Permissions Policy, HSTS and cache controls require real HTTP response headers.
+
+Before release, confirm response headers on HTML routes, assets and error responses using deployment tooling or an HTTP client. Check host access/security log settings, restrict operator access, avoid recording query strings, and set the shortest retention needed for operations. This repository cannot enforce or promise host-level log deletion. Do not publish secrets or personal information in static files, URLs, build output, or logs.
+
+## Future data handling
+
+Account, rewards and ordering routes are placeholders; this repository has no authentication, session handling, payment processing or data store. The misleading GET logout link was removed because there is no session to end. Any future logout or other state-changing endpoint must use a server-validated method and CSRF protection.
+
+Before adding collection, define necessary fields and deletion periods, publish an accurate notice, and keep optional marketing off by default. Validate inputs on the server, authorize every record access, redact logs, encrypt data in transit and at rest, and keep credentials server-side. Use Secure, HttpOnly, appropriately SameSite session cookies rather than browser storage for tokens. Minimize caching of personal responses and provide deletion controls. Revise CSP narrowly for reviewed integrations; never enable broad wildcard sources or inline script execution to bypass it.
